    }
   ],
   "estimatedMinutes": 345,
   "handsOnLab": {
    "title": "Verify probability empirically in Python",
    "brief": "You will verify the binomial probabilities, Bayes' computation, and percentile values in this module by direct computation and simulation in Python.",
    "steps": [
     "Recompute mean/median/pstdev of the latency data with statistics; assert equality with the notes.",
     "Write binom_pmf(n,k,p) with math.comb; assert the k=0..10 values sum to 1 within 1e-9.",
     "Simulate 200,000 trials of 'exactly 3 of 10 at p=0.5' with random; compare the empirical rate to the theoretical value.",
     "Recompute the Bayes posterior with the given numbers; assert it matches the notes.",
     "Implement the percentile function and assert p50/p99 match; then compute p99.9 on a larger synthetic sample.",
     "Write the verdict report."
    ],
    "deliverable": "stats_check.py with asserts plus the verdict report.",
    "checkpoint": "Simulation within 0.005 of theory; all asserts pass.",
    "id": "il-05-cloud-devsecops-platform-sre-math-stats--lab"
   },
   "id": "il-05-cloud-devsecops-platform-sre-math-stats",
   "mockTask": {
    "q": "A detector flags 5% of benign traffic and catches 99% of attacks. Attacks are 1% of traffic. Roughly what fraction of flags are real attacks?",
    "choices": [
     "~17%",
     "~99%",
     "~50%",
     "~5%"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explain": "Bayes with base rates: only ~17% of flags are real — the base-rate effect.",
    "id": "il-05-cloud-devsecops-platform-sre-math-stats--mock",
    "type": "choice"
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Describing data, computed",
     "body": "Worked example on latency samples (ms): [12, 15, 18, 22, 22, 27, 31, 35, 40, 48]. Mean = Σx/n = 270/10 = **27**. Median (middle value) = **24.5**. Population std dev = **10.91**. Mean − median gap signals skew: the 48 drags the mean above the median. Rule: report center AND spread together; a mean without a spread is half a fact. SLOs, error budgets, and capacity planning are applied statistics."
    },
    {
     "title": "Probability rules",
     "body": "P(A and B) = P(A)·P(B|A); for independent events, P(A)·P(B). P(A or B) = P(A)+P(B)−P(A and B). Worked example: two independent deploys each fail with p=0.1. P(both fail) = 0.1×0.1 = **0.010000000000000002**. P(at least one fails) = 1 − P(neither) = 1 − 0.9² = **0.19**. Complement-first ('1 minus the boring case') solves most 'at least one' problems. Independence is an assumption to check, not a default: correlated failures break the multiplication."
    },
    {
     "title": "Conditional probability and Bayes",
     "body": "Bayes: P(D|+) = P(+|D)·P(D) / P(+). Worked example: 1% of requests are malicious (0.01); the detector catches 99% of malicious and flags 5% of benign. P(+) = 0.99×0.01 + 0.05×0.99 = **0.0594**. So P(malicious | flagged) = 0.0099/0.0594 = **0.167** — only about 1 in 6 flags is real. This is the base-rate effect: with rare events, even good tests produce mostly false positives. Every alert-fatigue argument in operations is Bayes in disguise."
    },
    {
     "title": "Expectation",
     "body": "E[X] = Σ x·P(x): the long-run average. Worked example: a retry policy succeeds on attempt 1 with p=0.7, attempt 2 with p=0.2, attempt 3 with p=0.1 (then gives up). Expected attempts = 1×0.7 + 2×0.2 + 3×0.1 = **1.4000000000000001**. Linearity of expectation: E[X+Y] = E[X]+E[Y] even when X,Y are dependent — the workhorse for analyzing total cost, total latency, total errors across components without modeling their joint distribution."
    },
    {
     "title": "The binomial distribution, computed",
     "body": "P(X=k) = C(n,k)·p^k·(1−p)^(n−k). Worked example: 10 requests, each fails independently with p=0.5 (a coin). P(exactly 3 fail) = C(10,3)×0.5^3×0.5^7 = 120×0.5^10 = **0.1172**. Sanity: probabilities over k=0..10 sum to 1.000000 = 1 ✓. Use the binomial for 'k of n independent trials' questions — canary failures, bit errors, A/B conversions."
    },
    {
     "title": "Percentiles and SLO math",
     "body": "Worked on response times (ms): [22, 25, 28, 31, 33, 35, 38, 41, 45, 52, 61, 78, 120, 210, 400]. Sorted already. p50 (median) = **41** ms; p99 = **373.4** ms. The mean would be polluted by the 400 ms outlier; percentiles describe what users actually feel. SLO connection: a '99.9% of requests < 300 ms' objective is a percentile statement — an error budget of 0.1% means 0.015 bad requests allowed per 15 in this sample's scale. Measure percentiles on real data, never assume normality for latency."
    }
   ],
   "objectives": [
    "Summarize data with center and spread, choosing robust measures deliberately.",
    "Apply Bayes' theorem and explain the base-rate effect.",
    "Compute binomial probabilities and expected values.",
    "Use percentiles for latency/SLO reasoning instead of means."
   ],
