   "id": "il-05-cloud-devsecops-platform-sre-math-graphs",
   "mockTask": {
    "q": "Why does Dijkstra's greedy choice work?",
    "choices": [
     "Non-negative weights: the closest unsettled vertex can't be improved",
     "Graphs are always DAGs",
     "BFS already ran",
     "Weights are integers"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explain": "Any alternative path to it would be at least as long.",
    "id": "il-05-cloud-devsecops-platform-sre-math-graphs--mock",
    "type": "choice"
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Graphs and the handshaking lemma",
     "body": "Worked graph: vertices [0, 1, 2, 3, 4], edges [(0, 1), (0, 2), (1, 2), (1, 3), (2, 4), (3, 4)]. Degrees: {0: 2, 1: 3, 2: 3, 3: 2, 4: 2}. Sum of degrees = **12**; 2×|E| = 2×6 = **12** — the handshaking lemma holds: every edge contributes 2 to the degree sum. Corollary: the number of odd-degree vertices is always even. Networks, dependency maps, and IaC resource graphs are all graphs; the lemma is your first consistency check on any degree data."
    },
    {
     "title": "Representations: lists vs matrices",
     "body": "Adjacency list of vertex 1: **[0, 2, 3]**. Adjacency matrix M has M[i][j]=1 iff edge (i,j): row 1 = **[1, 0, 1, 1, 0]**. Lists win for sparse graphs (few edges); matrices win for dense ones and for algebraic tricks (M² counts 2-walks). Worked: (M²)[0][4] counts length-2 walks 0→4: via 2 only → **1**. Choose the representation that fits the density and the operation."
    },
    {
     "title": "BFS, computed",
     "body": "BFS from vertex 0 visits neighbors level by level. Worked order: **[0, 1, 2, 3, 4]**. Level 0: {0}; level 1: {1,2}; level 2: {3,4}. BFS discovers shortest-path distances in unweighted graphs: dist(0→4) = **4** edges (0→2→4). This is the algorithm behind 'degrees of separation', network reachability, and dependency-level builds."
    },
    {
     "title": "Dijkstra on weighted graphs, computed",
     "body": "Edge weights: {(0, 1): 4, (0, 2): 2, (1, 2): 1, (1, 3): 5, (2, 4): 10, (3, 4): 3}. Dijkstra from 0: distances = **{0: 0, 1: 3, 2: 2, 3: 8, 4: 11}**. Trace: 0 (d=0) relaxes 1→4, 2→2; extract 2 (d=2) relaxes 1→3, 4→12; extract 1 (d=3) relaxes 3→8; extract 3 (d=8) relaxes 4→11; final dist(0→4) = **11** via 0→2→1→3→4. Greedy works because weights are non-negative — the closest unsettled vertex can never be improved later. This is routing, literally."
    },
    {
     "title": "DAGs and topological order",
     "body": "A DAG (directed acyclic graph) has a topological order: every edge goes forward. Worked: tasks A→B, A→C, B→D, C→D. One valid order: **A, B, C, D** (B,C interchangeable). Kahn's algorithm: repeatedly emit a vertex with no remaining incoming edges. Build pipelines, Terraform dependency graphs, and course prerequisites are DAGs — topological order is the execution plan. A cycle means deadlock: no valid order exists, and the cycle itself is the diagnosis."
    },
    {
     "title": "Trees",
     "body": "A tree on n vertices has exactly n−1 edges and exactly one path between any two vertices. Worked: our 5-vertex graph has 6 edges — not a tree (one extra edge creates exactly one cycle: 0−1−2−0). Remove edge (1,2): 5 vertices, 5−1 = 4 edges → a tree. Spanning trees connect everything cheapest-first (Kruskal/Prim); running Kruskal on the weighted graph takes edges (1,2):1, (0,2):2, (3,4):3, (1,3):5, total cost **11** — computed, including the skipped (0,1):4 which would close the cycle 0−1−2−0. Networks, service meshes, and IaC dependency graphs are graphs; traverse them with these algorithms."
    }
   ],
