   },
   "id": "il05-devops-core",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "il05-devops-core--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Jenkins security gate placement?",
    "choices": [
     "after deploy",
     "never",
     "manual quarterly",
     "in the pipeline before push/deploy — fail the build"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "Shift-left: SAST/dependency scans gate the pipeline; failing the build beats failing the audit. — see 'Hands-on procedure' in this module's notes."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The vocabulary of the toolchain — every term here appears in the objectives, the procedure, and the quiz.\n- **DevOps roles** — build, release, operate, observe, secure, automate, collaborate; the seven roles whose boundaries vary by org and give this path its sequence.\n- **trunk-based branching** — short-lived branches merged straight to main; fits small teams and fast cadences.\n- **GitFlow** — a branching model with release trains and more ceremony; fits scheduled releases, at the cost of overhead.\n- **monorepo** — one repository for many services; buys atomic cross-service changes, costs tooling complexity.\n- **polyrepo** — one repository per service; buys team autonomy, costs versioning and coordination overhead.\n- **DORA metrics** — the industry-standard delivery performance signals; review latency is one of them in disguise.\n- **Docker image** — a packaged, immutable filesystem-plus-metadata unit built from a Dockerfile.\n- **Docker layer** — each Dockerfile instruction creates a cached layer; ordering instructions for cache hits is a core skill.\n- **multi-stage build** — compile in a fat build stage, copy only the artifact into a slim runtime stage; an 800MB build environment becomes a ~15MB image.\n- **registry** — where images live; tagging discipline means immutable tags for releases.\n- **pipeline as code** — the Jenkins pipeline defined in a Jenkinsfile, versioned with the repo.\n- **security scanning gate** — SAST and dependency scans placed in the pipeline before push/deploy; the build fails, not the audit.\n- **SBOM** — software bill of materials; the inventory of what's inside an artifact.\n- **Kubernetes pod** — the smallest deployable unit: one or more containers sharing network and storage.\n- **deployment** — the workload controller that runs rolling updates of pods.\n- **ConfigMap** — non-sensitive configuration injected as env vars or files.\n- **Kubernetes secret** — base64-encoded (not encrypted) configuration; the real security boundary is etcd encryption plus RBAC.\n- **ingress** — the cluster's HTTP(S) front door, routing external traffic to services.\n- **Helm chart** — a package of Kubernetes manifests; charts are the packaging format.\n- **Ansible playbook** — the idempotent fleet-state definition; roles organize reusable playbooks.\n- **ansible-vault** — encrypts secret variables at rest in version control; distinct from HashiCorp Vault.\n- **PromQL** — the Prometheus query language; `rate(http_requests_total[5m])` is the per-second request rate over a 5-minute window.\n- **RED method** — Rate, Errors, Duration: the request-centric dashboard method.\n- **USE method** — Utilization, Saturation, Errors: the resource-centric dashboard method.\n- **readiness probe** — gates traffic to a pod; zero-downtime rolling updates are a probe configuration."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Prove each tool against a running system, in adoption order. You will containerize a small web app, gate it through a pipeline, deploy it to Kubernetes, and observe it — the milestone arc from this module.\n1. **Containerize.** Write a multi-stage Dockerfile: `FROM golang:1.22 AS build` compiles the app, then `FROM gcr.io/distroless/static` ships only the binary. Put slow-changing instructions (dependency install) early and `COPY . .` last, so source edits don't bust the dependency cache.\n2. **Pipeline.** Write a Jenkinsfile with stages lint, test, build, scan, push, deploy — with a gate between each. Place the SAST/dependency scan before push so a vulnerable artifact never reaches the registry; store the SBOM with the artifact.\n3. **Deploy.** Apply a Deployment with a readiness probe, expose it with a Service and Ingress, and externalize config into a ConfigMap (secrets into a Secret, knowing they're base64). Roll the Deployment and watch the readiness probe hold traffic off new pods until they answer.\n4. **Observe.** Point Prometheus at the app, query `rate(http_requests_total[5m])` in PromQL, build a Grafana dashboard on the RED method, and add one alert: page on user-facing symptoms (latency, error rate), ticket on causes (disk filling).\n5. **Fleet state.** Write an Ansible playbook that converges a VM to the same baseline idempotently; store its secrets with ansible-vault.",
     "code": "# Multi-stage Dockerfile: compile fat, ship slim\nFROM golang:1.22 AS build\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY go.mod go.sum ./\nRUN go mod download        # slow-changing: cached\nCOPY . .                   # fast-changing: last\nRUN go build -o server .\n\nFROM gcr.io/distroless/static\nCOPY --from=build /app/server /server\nENTRYPOINT [\"/server\"]\n\n# Jenkinsfile stage skeleton with a security gate\npipeline {\n  agent any\n  stages {\n    stage('lint')   { steps { sh 'make lint' } }\n    stage('test')   { steps { sh 'make test' } }\n    stage('build')  { steps { sh 'docker build -t app:${BUILD_ID} .' } }\n    stage('scan')   { steps { sh 'trivy image app:${BUILD_ID}' } } // gate: fail the build\n    stage('push')   { steps { sh 'docker push app:${BUILD_ID}' } }\n    stage('deploy') { steps { sh 'kubectl set image deploy/app app=app:${BUILD_ID}' } }\n  }\n}",
     "lang": "bash"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Ship the milestone arc end to end: a containerized service, built by a Jenkins pipeline with a security gate, deployed to Kubernetes with zero-downtime rolling updates, observed in Grafana with a RED dashboard and one paging alert. Deliverables: the multi-stage Dockerfile, the Jenkinsfile, the Kubernetes manifests (Deployment, Service, Ingress, ConfigMap/Secret), and a screenshot or export of the dashboard. Write a one-page runbook answering: where does a failed security scan stop the flow, and what does the readiness probe protect during a rollout? This is the portfolio artifact that proves the whole module — each tool demonstrated by a running system, not by notes."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Dockerfile: where does COPY . . go?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Dockerfile: where does COPY . . go?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** last, after dependency install\n- **Why:** Source changes fastest — put it last so dependency layers stay cached. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Source changes fastest — put it last so dependency layers stay cached.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in DevOps at Cloud-Native Parity:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in DevOps at Cloud-Native Parity goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
