   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The certification-stack vocabulary — KCNA terms first, then the CKA/CKAD/CKS depth.\n- **control plane** — the cluster brain: API server, scheduler, controller manager, etcd.\n- **kubelet** — the node agent that runs pods as the control plane instructs.\n- **etcd** — the cluster's source of truth; all state lives here. Back it up with `etcdctl snapshot save`.\n- **resource request** — the scheduling guarantee; the scheduler places pods by requests.\n- **resource limit** — the enforcement ceiling; CPU throttles, memory kills (OOMKilled).\n- **taint / toleration** — taints repel pods from nodes; tolerations let specific pods schedule anyway.\n- **affinity** — scheduling preference rules (node or pod affinity/anti-affinity).\n- **ClusterIP / NodePort / LoadBalancer** — the service types: internal-only, node-port exposure, cloud load balancer.\n- **CNI** — the container network interface layer; know it at concept level for CKA.\n- **PV / PVC** — persistent volume (the storage) and persistent volume claim (the request); StorageClasses provision dynamically.\n- **init container** — runs to completion before app containers start; the CKAD setup hook.\n- **liveness probe** — restarts the container when it fails.\n- **readiness probe** — removes the pod from service endpoints when it fails; no restart.\n- **startup probe** — guards slow starters so liveness doesn't kill them mid-boot.\n- **Pod Security Standards** — privileged, baseline, restricted; each level forbids more.\n- **admission control** — the policy enforcement point where supply-chain rules actually bite.\n- **cosign** — image signing; signed images plus admission enforcement is the supply-chain gate.\n- **Falco** — runtime threat detection on syscalls; detects anomalous behavior in running containers.\n- **seccomp** — syscall filtering profiles that constrain what a compromised container can invoke.\n- **AppArmor** — mandatory access control profiles constraining process capabilities.\n- **KCSA** — the associate-level cloud-native security exam; the on-ramp before CKS."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Drill the exact motions the performance exams grade: speed, triage order, and backup discipline.\n1. **Generate, don't hand-write.** Build manifests imperatively: `kubectl create deploy web --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > deploy.yaml`, then `kubectl expose deploy web --port=80 --dry-run=client -o yaml >> deploy.yaml`. Aliases and `--dry-run=client -o yaml` are the exam skill.\n2. **Triage in fixed order.** Break a cluster, then fix it the same way every time: `kubectl describe` the object, `kubectl logs` the container, read events, then check the kubelet. Troubleshooting is the CKA's largest hidden domain.\n3. **Back up etcd.** Run `etcdctl snapshot save` with the right flags against a test cluster, then practice the restore — the restore is the tested skill.\n4. **Probe lab.** Deploy an app with misconfigured liveness and readiness probes; observe the restart loop versus the traffic blackhole, then fix each. Misconfigured probes are the #1 self-inflicted outage.\n5. **Harden.** Apply a restricted Pod Security Standard, add a seccomp profile, write a Falco rule alerting on an unexpected shell in a container, and require signed images at admission with an ImagePolicyWebhook-style gate.",
     "code": "# Imperative speed: generate YAML, don't hand-write it\nkubectl create deploy web --image=nginx --dry-run=client -o yaml > deploy.yaml\nkubectl expose deploy web --port=80 --dry-run=client -o yaml >> deploy.yaml\nkubectl set image deploy/web web=nginx:1.27  # rollout\nkubectl rollout status deploy/web\n\n# Triage order: describe -> logs -> events -> kubelet\nkubectl describe pod <pod>\nkubectl logs <pod> --previous\nkubectl get events --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp\n\n# etcd backup (know the flags; restore is the tested skill)\nETCDCTL_API=3 etcdctl snapshot save /tmp/etcd-backup.db \\\n  --endpoints=https://127.0.0.1:2379 \\\n  --cacert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/ca.crt \\\n  --cert=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.crt \\\n  --key=/etc/kubernetes/pki/etcd/server.key",
     "lang": "bash"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Build the 'broken cluster' lab and its runbook: provision a test cluster, inject five faults (bad image, failed probe, resource exhaustion, bad network policy, corrupted etcd member — with a snapshot taken first), and fix each using the fixed triage order, timing yourself. Deliverables: the fault-injection script or checklist, the triage runbook (describe → logs → events → kubelet), and a hardening checklist applied afterward (restricted PSS, seccomp, one Falco rule, signed-image admission). This is the CKA/CKS muscle memory — speed with kubectl under a 2-hour clock, with docs allowed."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: etcd data lost, no backup. Outcome?",
     "body": "**Problem.** etcd data lost, no backup. Outcome?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** cluster state lost — rebuild\n- **Why:** etcd IS the cluster state. No backup = rebuild. Back it up. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *etcd IS the cluster state.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in Cloud-Native: The Kubernetes Certification Stack:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in Cloud-Native: The Kubernetes Certification Stack goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
