   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "The three logical components?",
     "back": "Policy Engine (decides), Policy Administrator (executes), Policy Enforcement Point (enforces)."
    },
    {
     "front": "Control plane vs data plane?",
     "back": "Control: policy decisions. Data: the traffic itself. Separate them."
    },
    {
     "front": "Phishing-resistant MFA = ?",
     "back": "FIDO2/WebAuthn — cryptographic, origin-bound. SMS/TOTP are phishable."
    },
    {
     "front": "Three deployment models?",
     "back": "Device agent/gateway, enclave (per-resource PEPs), resource portal."
    },
    {
     "front": "Microsegmentation secures which traffic?",
     "back": "East-west (workload-to-workload) — the lateral-movement surface."
    },
    {
     "front": "Migration first step?",
     "back": "Inventory and classify — then pilot on the highest-value resources."
    },
    {
     "front": "PE vs PA vs PEP?",
     "back": "Policy Engine decides, Policy Administrator executes (provisions/configures), Policy Enforcement Point enforces (the gate)."
    },
    {
     "front": "Why separate control and data planes?",
     "back": "Decisions (control) must be isolated from traffic (data) — compromise of one must not imply the other."
    },
    {
     "front": "MFA strength ladder?",
     "back": "FIDO2 > TOTP app > SMS (SIM-swap) > none. Phishing resistance is the criterion."
    },
    {
     "front": "SDP in one line?",
     "back": "Authenticate first, connect second — the network is dark until identity and posture verify."
    },
    {
     "front": "Per-session access grants implement which tenet?",
     "back": "**per-session least privilege** — Tenet 3: access is granted per session to least privilege — no standing trust. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes."
    },
    {
     "front": "PEP placement in the enclave model?",
     "back": "**per-resource gateways** — Enclave: each resource (or group) gets its own PEP — fine-grained enforcement. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes."
    }
