   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The SP 800-207 vocabulary, exactly as the tenets and components define it.\n- **zero trust** — 'never trust, always verify'; access granted per session to least privilege, never by network location.\n- **tenet 1** — all data sources and services are resources.\n- **tenet 2** — all communication is secured regardless of network location.\n- **tenet 3** — access to resources is granted on a per-session basis.\n- **tenet 4** — access is determined by dynamic policy: identity, device, and behavior.\n- **tenet 5** — the enterprise monitors the integrity and security posture of all assets.\n- **tenet 6** — authentication and authorization are dynamic and strictly enforced before access.\n- **tenet 7** — the enterprise collects maximum information to improve its security posture.\n- **Policy Engine (PE)** — the component that decides: evaluates policy and the trust algorithm.\n- **Policy Administrator (PA)** — the component that executes: provisions credentials and configures the PEP.\n- **Policy Enforcement Point (PEP)** — the gate where the decision is enforced: gateway, agent, or enclave.\n- **trust algorithm** — the PE's decision logic: static rules versus dynamic behavioral scoring; policy logic, not ML magic.\n- **control plane** — where policy decisions are made; separated from the data plane.\n- **data plane** — where the traffic actually flows.\n- **phishing-resistant MFA** — FIDO2/WebAuthn: cryptographic, origin-bound authentication. The MFA strength ladder is FIDO2 > TOTP app > SMS (SIM-swappable) > none.\n- **device posture** — managed, patched, encrypted, EDR-healthy; a policy input that automatically reduces access for unhealthy devices.\n- **continuous authentication** — re-verification on risk signals, not just at login.\n- **privileged access workstation (PAW)** — hardened admin tiering; never administer the kingdom from a browsing laptop.\n- **microsegmentation** — workload-to-workload policy on east-west traffic.\n- **software-defined perimeter (SDP)** — authenticate first, connect second; the network is dark until identity and posture verify.\n- **east-west traffic** — internal workload-to-workload traffic; the lateral-movement surface.\n- **north-south traffic** — traffic entering or leaving the perimeter.\n- **device agent/gateway model** — a deployment model with an agent or gateway as the PEP.\n- **enclave model** — a deployment model with per-resource (or per-group) PEPs.\n- **resource portal model** — a deployment model with a gateway fronting legacy resources.\n- **CISA zero-trust maturity model** — traditional → advanced → optimal, across pillars."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Turn the tenets into an architecture review you can run on any estate. Work it as a procedure, not a slogan.\n1. **Inventory and classify.** List resources and data flows; rank by value. Migration starts at the crown jewels — inventory and classification are step one, always.\n2. **Draw the decision path.** For one critical resource, diagram subject → PEP → PA/PE: where is the enforcement point, what does the trust algorithm evaluate (identity, device posture, behavior), and what does the PA configure on grant?\n3. **Identity-first access design.** Specify the MFA ladder per tier (FIDO2 for privileged and high-value, TOTP minimum elsewhere), device-posture inputs, and just-in-time privileged elevation with session recording.\n4. **Microsegment one flow.** Write the east-west policy for a single workload pair (allow only the required ports/protocols, default deny), and require mTLS between the services.\n5. **Pick the deployment model.** Match agent/gateway, enclave, or resource portal to the resource's constraints (managed devices? legacy app? per-resource sensitivity?) and justify the PEP placement.\n6. **Measure.** Define the migration metrics before starting: % of access decisions via PE/PEP, % phishing-resistant MFA enrollment, mean time to grant/revoke, and lateral-movement test results."
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Produce the zero-trust migration plan for a fictional 500-person company with one legacy datacenter app and a growing SaaS footprint. Deliverables: the tenet-to-control map (each of the seven tenets mapped to concrete controls), the PE/PA/PEP diagram for the highest-value resource, the identity design (MFA ladder, device posture, privileged access), one microsegmentation policy, the chosen deployment model with PEP-placement rationale, and the phased migration sequence (inventory → classify → pilot → expand) with the CISA maturity steps marked. Grade it on rationale: two good architects can disagree on the model, but both must defend the fit."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Per-session access grants implement which tenet?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Per-session access grants implement which tenet?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** per-session least privilege\n- **Why:** Tenet 3: access is granted per session to least privilege — no standing trust. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Tenet 3: access is granted per session to least privilege — no standing trust.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in Zero-Trust Platform Architecture:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in Zero-Trust Platform Architecture goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
