    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The HCL and state vocabulary the Associate exam is built on.\n- **resource** — a `resource \"aws_instance\" \"web\"` block declaring desired state.\n- **provider** — the plugin that talks to an API (AWS, Azure, and others).\n- **data source** — reads existing infrastructure into config; the brownfield bridge.\n- **variable / output / local** — the parameterization trio: inputs, exposed values, internal expressions.\n- **init** — installs providers and modules; the workflow's first step.\n- **validate** — checks syntax and internal consistency.\n- **plan** — computes the diff; the code review of infrastructure. Read it — never auto-apply blind in production.\n- **apply** — executes the planned changes.\n- **state** — the mapping of config to real resources; lose it and Terraform forgets your infrastructure.\n- **remote state** — shared, locked, versioned state (S3 + DynamoDB/S3-native locking, Terraform Cloud, GCS).\n- **state locking** — serializes applies; concurrent applies without locking corrupt state.\n- **import** — `terraform import aws_instance.web i-12345` binds brownfield to state; you must then write matching config.\n- **moved block** — refactors resources (rename, module shift) without destroy/recreate.\n- **drift** — real infrastructure diverging from config; apply converges it after you review the plan.\n- **taint / replace** — marks a resource for recreation.\n- **module** — a composable unit: root module calls child modules via `source`; inputs via variables, outputs bubble up.\n- **workspace** — `terraform workspace new prod`: same config, separate states; fine for small setups, separate configs for serious multi-env.\n- **count vs for_each** — count for identical numbered instances (fragile: removing the middle shifts indices and recreates); for_each for named instances with stable keys.\n- **remote state data source** — shares outputs between configurations; composition over duplication."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Run the workflow the way production demands: plan as code review, state as crown jewels.\n1. **Init and validate.** `terraform init`, then `terraform validate` — providers installed, syntax clean.\n2. **Plan to a file.** `terraform plan -out=tfplan`; read the diff like a code review: every create/update/destroy must be intentional. The saved plan is the auditable artifact.\n3. **Apply the saved plan.** `terraform apply tfplan` — never `apply` without the reviewed plan in production.\n4. **Remote state with locking.** Configure the backend (S3 + DynamoDB/S3-native locking or Terraform Cloud) so concurrent applies serialize instead of corrupting state. Never hand-edit state except via `terraform state mv`/`rm` — and back it up first.\n5. **Import brownfield.** `terraform import` the existing resource, then write the matching config immediately — import only binds state; the config must describe it.\n6. **Refactor safely.** Use `moved` blocks to rename or shift modules without destroy/recreate; run `terraform fmt` and `validate` in CI like app code.\n7. **Compose.** Split a VPC + compute stack into child modules with clear inputs/outputs; share outputs between configurations via the remote state data source.",
     "code": "# The disciplined workflow: plan to a file, review, then apply\nterraform init\nterraform validate\nterraform fmt -check\nterraform plan -out=tfplan     # READ THE DIFF — the code review of infra\nterraform apply tfplan        # apply the reviewed plan, never blind\n\n# Remote state with locking (the crown jewels, protected)\nterraform {\n  backend \"s3\" {\n    bucket         = \"tf-state-prod\"\n    key            = \"network/terraform.tfstate\"\n    region         = \"us-east-1\"\n    dynamodb_table = \"tf-state-locks\"\n    encrypt        = true\n  }\n}\n\n# Brownfield: import first, then write the matching config\nterraform import aws_instance.web i-12345\n\n# Refactor without destroy/recreate\nmoved {\n  from = aws_instance.web\n  to   = module.compute.aws_instance.web\n}",
     "lang": "hcl"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Build the module's reference stack: a composable Terraform project with a thin root module calling VPC and compute child modules, remote state with locking, and a CI check running fmt and validate. Deliverables: the module tree with inputs/outputs documented, the backend configuration, one `moved`-block refactor demonstrated, and a brownfield import (a manually created resource brought under management with matching config). Include a one-page exam-day sheet: the workflow order, the state commands, and the 'what happens when' answers (drift, lock contention, count-index removal) reasoned from the state model."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: apply without plan in production?",
     "body": "**Problem.** apply without plan in production?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** reckless — always review the plan\n- **Why:** The plan is the diff review of infrastructure. Blind apply is how outages are born. — see 'Hands-on procedure' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *The plan is the diff review of infrastructure.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in Terraform: Infrastructure as Code:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in Terraform: Infrastructure as Code goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
