   "cards": [
    {
     "front": "Multi-AZ vs multi-instance?",
     "back": "Instances in one AZ share a failure domain. Multi-AZ is the resilience unit."
    },
    {
     "front": "Decoupling benefit?",
     "back": "Failures become queue depth, not errors — consumers retry instead of requests failing."
    },
    {
     "front": "SG vs NACL?",
     "back": "Security groups: stateful, instance-level. NACLs: stateless, subnet-level, explicit both directions."
    },
    {
     "front": "DynamoDB vs Aurora?",
     "back": "Key-value at scale → DynamoDB. Complex relational joins → Aurora. Query pattern decides."
    },
    {
     "front": "S3 Intelligent-Tiering?",
     "back": "Auto-moves objects between access tiers — for unpredictable access patterns."
    },
    {
     "front": "Spot vs Reserved?",
     "back": "Spot: interruptible, cheapest — batch/fault-tolerant. Reserved/SP: steady-state commitment discount."
    },
    {
     "front": "Cache invalidation strategy?",
     "back": "TTLs plus versioned keys — one of the two hard problems, handled explicitly."
    },
    {
     "front": "SAA-C03 scenario tiebreaker?",
     "back": "Re-read the constraints: cost? resilience? ops overhead? The constraint picks the answer."
    },
    {
     "front": "Reference stacks?",
     "back": "Composable Terraform modules (VPC, ALB+ASG, RDS, S3+CloudFront) — every architecture as IaC."
    },
    {
     "front": "Data transfer cost awareness?",
     "back": "Same-AZ traffic free; cross-region metered. Architecture choices have a transfer bill."
    },
    {
     "front": "ALB in 2 AZs, instances in 1 AZ. Resilient?",
     "back": "**no — instances share one AZ's fate** — The ALB survives AZ loss; the instances don't. Resilience needs multi-AZ at every tier. — see 'Hands-on procedure' in this module's notes."
    },
    {
     "front": "Order service calls inventory synchronously; inventory slowdowns cascade. Fix?",
     "back": "**SQS between them — decouple** — Decouple with a queue: slowdowns become queue depth, retries replace errors. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes."
    }
   ],
