    "explain": "Decouple with a queue: slowdowns become queue depth, retries replace errors. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The architect's vocabulary — the four SAA pillars in glossary form.\n- **Availability Zone (AZ)** — an isolated failure domain; multi-AZ (not just multi-instance) is the resilience unit.\n- **decoupling** — SQS/SNS/EventBridge between tiers; turns cascading failures into queue depth, because a failed consumer retries instead of a failed request erroring.\n- **RTO / RPO** — recovery time / recovery point objectives; the business numbers that choose the DR pattern.\n- **DR patterns** — backup-restore → pilot light → warm standby → multi-site active-active; cost and recovery speed rise together.\n- **ALB** — application load balancer, spread across AZs.\n- **RDS Multi-AZ** — standby replica in another AZ for failover.\n- **S3 durability** — the 11-9s design target.\n- **compute selection** — EC2 vs Lambda vs Fargate vs ECS, chosen by workload shape.\n- **storage selection** — EBS gp3/io2, EFS, S3, instance store, chosen by access pattern.\n- **Aurora** — the relational choice for complex joins and transactions.\n- **DynamoDB** — the key-value choice at scale.\n- **caching** — CloudFront and ElastiCache; cache at every layer.\n- **elasticity** — Auto Scaling on the right metric.\n- **security group** — stateful, instance-level; return traffic is allowed automatically.\n- **NACL** — stateless, subnet-level; explicit rules in both directions — the classic exam trap.\n- **KMS** — envelope encryption with customer-managed keys (CMKs).\n- **permission boundary** — caps the maximum permissions a delegated identity can get.\n- **WAF / Shield** — web-application firewall and DDoS protection.\n- **pricing models** — On-Demand vs Reserved vs Spot vs Savings Plans, chosen by workload steadiness.\n- **S3 tiers** — Standard → Intelligent-Tiering → Glacier, chosen by access recency; Intelligent-Tiering auto-optimizes unpredictable patterns.\n- **right-sizing** — Compute Optimizer-driven instance fit; data transfer awareness (same-AZ free, cross-region metered)."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Design like the exam grades: constraints first, then services.\n1. **State RTO/RPO.** Write the business numbers down first; they choose the DR pattern (backup-restore through multi-site active).\n2. **Multi-AZ every tier.** Place the ALB across AZs and the instances across AZs too — an ALB in 2 AZs with instances in 1 AZ is not resilient.\n3. **Decouple.** Put SQS between any two tiers where a slowdown must not cascade; monitor queue depth, not just the app.\n4. **Choose by pattern.** Database: complex joins → Aurora; key-value at scale → DynamoDB. Compute and storage by workload shape and access pattern.\n5. **Secure the layers.** Least-privilege IAM (roles, not users, for apps), KMS envelope encryption, security groups for stateful instance rules and NACLs with explicit both-direction rules at the subnet.\n6. **Cost it.** Baseline on Savings Plans/Reserved, peaks on On-Demand/Spot; tier S3 by access recency; set TTLs and versioned keys for cache invalidation.\n7. **Codify.** Express the design as the Terraform reference stacks: VPC, ALB+ASG, RDS, S3+CloudFront as composable modules.\n8. **Eliminate by constraint.** On scenario questions with two plausible answers, re-read for the deciding constraint — cost, resilience, or ops overhead — and let it pick.",
     "code": "# Reference stack sketch: VPC + ALB + ASG + RDS (composable modules)\nmodule \"vpc\" {\n  source = \"./modules/vpc\"   # multi-AZ subnets, NAT, route tables\n}\nmodule \"web\" {\n  source     = \"./modules/alb-asg\"\n  subnet_ids = module.vpc.public_subnet_ids  # ALB across AZs\n}\nmodule \"db\" {\n  source          = \"./modules/rds\"\n  multi_az        = true                     # resilience unit = AZ\n  subnet_ids      = module.vpc.private_subnet_ids\n}\n# Decouple tiers: slowdowns become queue depth\nresource \"aws_sqs_queue\" \"orders\" {\n  name = \"orders\"\n}",
     "lang": "hcl"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Design a three-tier web application for a fictional retailer: state the RTO/RPO, draw the multi-AZ architecture with a decoupled order pipeline (SQS between web and fulfillment), justify the database choice by query pattern, apply the security layering (IAM roles, KMS, SG vs NACL), and cost the steady baseline plus spiky peaks across pricing models. Deliverables: the architecture diagram, the constraint log (every major choice tied to cost, resilience, or ops overhead), and the Terraform reference-stack modules (VPC, ALB+ASG, RDS, S3+CloudFront). This is the SAA muscle: defend the trade-offs, not just the choices."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: ALB in 2 AZs, instances in 1 AZ. Resilient?",
     "body": "**Problem.** ALB in 2 AZs, instances in 1 AZ. Resilient?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** no — instances share one AZ's fate\n- **Why:** The ALB survives AZ loss; the instances don't. Resilience needs multi-AZ at every tier. — see 'Hands-on procedure' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *The ALB survives AZ loss; the instances don't.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in AWS Solutions Architecture: SAA-C03:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in AWS Solutions Architecture: SAA-C03 goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
