   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The operator's glossary — every pair the exam loves to test.\n- **CloudFormation** — declarative infrastructure stacks; drift detection finds the console cowboys; StackSets extend to multi-account.\n- **blue/green deployment** — CodeDeploy shifts traffic between environments; the rollback story is the feature.\n- **Systems Manager** — Patch Manager, Run Command, Parameter Store: the operator's console.\n- **golden AMI** — baked, tested, distributed images via EC2 Image Builder; immutable infrastructure starts here.\n- **AWS Backup** — centralized backup policies with cross-region copy.\n- **Route 53 health checks + failover routing** — DNS steers away from the dead region.\n- **CloudTrail** — who did what: the API-activity audit log.\n- **Config** — what changed: resource state over time, with rules and remediation. The exam pairs Trail and Config constantly.\n- **IAM Access Analyzer** — finds unintended, over-permissive access; the least-privilege auditor.\n- **GuardDuty** — threat detection on the logs; findings feed EventBridge → Lambda/SSM for the automated response loop.\n- **Security Hub** — the aggregated security posture view.\n- **VPC Gateway endpoint** — S3/DynamoDB access off the internet; free, via a route-table entry.\n- **VPC Interface endpoint** — PrivateLink-powered access to other services; hourly charge.\n- **CloudFront signed URLs/cookies + OAC** — private content delivered through the CDN with authorized access.\n- **routing policies** — failover, latency-based, geoproximity: the Route 53 traffic shapes.\n- **Cost Explorer + Budgets** — spend visibility and guardrails.\n- **Compute Optimizer** — right-sizing recommendations.\n- **S3 lifecycle policies** — automatic tiering and expiry by age.\n- **gp3 over gp2** — cheaper and independently tunable.\n- **Graviton** — the price-performance processor option.\n- **untested backup** — a rumor; the RTO is only real if restores are proven on schedule."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Operate it like the exam's scenarios: deploy, back up, detect drift, respond.\n1. **Deploy blue/green.** Ship a CodeDeploy blue/green deployment; rehearse the rollback — the rollback story is the feature, and deploying without one is a hope.\n2. **Bake a golden AMI.** Use EC2 Image Builder to bake, test, and distribute an AMI; deploy from it so infrastructure stays immutable.\n3. **Back up to RTO/RPO.** Create an AWS Backup plan with cross-region copy, then actually test a restore — untested backups are rumors.\n4. **Wire the response loop.** Enable GuardDuty, route findings through EventBridge to a Lambda/SSM Automation remediation — detect to remediate without humans in the path.\n5. **Find the cowboys.** Run CloudFormation drift detection on a stack someone touched in the console; import or revert deliberately.\n6. **Audit access.** Run IAM Access Analyzer and remediate one over-permissive grant toward least privilege.\n7. **Cut the transfer bill.** Replace NAT-gateway S3 traffic with a Gateway VPC endpoint; serve private media via CloudFront signed URLs with Origin Access Control.",
     "code": "# Gateway VPC endpoint: S3 traffic stays on the AWS network (free)\naws ec2 create-vpc-endpoint \\\n  --vpc-id vpc-12345678 \\\n  --service-name com.amazonaws.us-east-1.s3 \\\n  --route-table-ids rtb-abcdef12\n\n# Drift detection: find the console cowboys\naws cloudformation detect-stack-drift --stack-name web-prod\naws cloudformation describe-stack-resource-drifts --stack-name web-prod\n\n# Who did what: recent API activity via CloudTrail lookup\naws cloudtrail lookup-events --lookup-attributes AttributeKey=EventName,AttributeValue=ConsoleLogin",
     "lang": "bash"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Build the operator's runbook pack for a fictional two-tier app: the blue/green deployment procedure with its rollback steps, the backup plan (RTO/RPO stated, cross-region copy, restore-test schedule), the GuardDuty → EventBridge → SSM response loop for one finding type, and the monthly ops checklist (drift detection, Access Analyzer review, Cost Explorer + Compute Optimizer pass). Deliverables: the runbook document, the CloudFormation template, and evidence of one tested restore. The SysOps exam grades runbook decisions — monitor → detect → respond — and so does this brief."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Console change bypassed CloudFormation. Detect?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Console change bypassed CloudFormation. Detect?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** drift detection\n- **Why:** Drift detection compares stack template to reality — finds the cowboys. — see 'Hands-on procedure' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Drift detection compares stack template to reality — finds the cowboys.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in AWS CloudOps: SysOps Administrator:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in AWS CloudOps: SysOps Administrator goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
