   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The professional-tier vocabulary.\n- **CodePipeline** — the AWS SDLC automation service: source → build → deploy stages.\n- **canary deployment** — release in percentage steps (10% → 50% → 100%) with metric gates and automatic rollback on regression.\n- **linear deployment** — release a fixed percentage every N minutes.\n- **all-at-once deployment** — fastest, riskiest; no safety net.\n- **feature flag** — decouples deploy from release; the safest deployment is the one you can turn off without redeploying.\n- **nested stack** — CloudFormation composition: stacks calling stacks.\n- **StackSet** — one template deployed across N accounts and regions; the org-scale deployment primitive and the landing-zone tool.\n- **drift governance** — detecting and reconciling template-vs-reality drift at scale.\n- **active-active multi-region** — Route 53 plus replicated data; compute is the easy part, data replication is the hard part.\n- **chaos thinking / Fault Injection Simulator (FIS)** — prove resilience with injected failures; resilience is proven, not asserted.\n- **CloudWatch composite alarm** — alarms on the AND of symptoms; reduces noise versus alarming on each symptom.\n- **Logs Insights** — query language over CloudWatch Logs (e.g. filter @message like /ERROR/).\n- **X-Ray** — distributed tracing; the service map draws itself — start every latency hunt there.\n- **SSM Automation document** — the executable runbook; incident response as code.\n- **artifact signing** — CodeSigner/signer Lambda provenance: deploy only signed artifacts; unsigned artifacts don't deploy.\n- **pipeline IAM role** — the pipeline's identity is a blast radius; scope it with least privilege to exactly the deploy actions.\n- **blameless post-incident review** — fix the system, not the person."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Ship the professional way: gated rollouts, traced systems, signed artifacts.\n1. **Pipeline.** Build a CodePipeline (source → build → deploy) with a scanning gate before the deploy stage — SAST, dependency, and container scans.\n2. **Canary.** Deploy with a canary strategy (10% → 50% → 100%), gating each step on canary-vs-baseline error rate and latency with automatic rollback on regression.\n3. **Flag it.** Wrap the new feature in a feature flag so a bad release is mitigated by flipping the flag — no redeploy.\n4. **Scale the IaC.** Convert the stack to nested stacks, then deploy it across two regions with a StackSet.\n5. **Trace.** Instrument with X-Ray; open the service map and run one Logs Insights query to find the slow dependency.\n6. **Game day.** Run a Fault Injection Simulator experiment against the multi-region setup and record what actually failed over.\n7. **Sign.** Add artifact signing to the pipeline; verify an unsigned artifact is rejected at the deploy stage.\n8. **Pace.** Drill 180-minute scenario blocks: eliminate by constraint, flag the 50/50s, keep moving.",
     "code": "# Logs Insights: find the errors fast\nfields @timestamp, @message\n| filter @message like /ERROR/\n| stats count() by bin(5m)\n\n# X-Ray: trace a request through 40 microservices\naws xray get-trace-summaries \\\n  --start-time $(date -u -d '1 hour ago' +%s) \\\n  --end-time $(date -u +%s) \\\n  --filter-expression 'service(\"checkout-api\") { error = true }'",
     "lang": "bash"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Deliver the professional release train for a fictional checkout service: a CodePipeline with scanning and signing gates, a canary deployment with metric gates and automatic rollback, feature flags on the risky paths, the stack expressed as nested stacks deployed via StackSet to two regions, X-Ray tracing with one documented latency hunt, and an FIS game-day report. Deliverables: the pipeline definition, the canary/rollback runbook, the game-day findings, and a pacing plan for the 180-minute exam. The brief is graded on safety at scale — every shortcut must have its gate."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Deploy to 1000 instances, catch regressions early?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Deploy to 1000 instances, catch regressions early?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** canary with metric gates\n- **Why:** Canary + automatic rollback on error/latency regression — safety at scale. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Canary + automatic rollback on error/latency regression — safety at scale.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in AWS DevOps Professional: DOP-C02:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in AWS DevOps Professional: DOP-C02 goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
