   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The specialty's glossary — detection, identity, data, and the landing zone.\n- **GuardDuty** — threat detection over the logs; produces findings.\n- **Detective** — investigation graphs: pivot from a finding to the entity to the timeline, in clicks not grep marathons.\n- **Security Hub** — aggregated security posture across accounts.\n- **Macie** — discovers and classifies PII in S3 at scale.\n- **org CloudTrail** — the multi-account API audit log; pair with an immutable bucket + Object Lock so attackers can't delete it.\n- **Config aggregator** — multi-account resource-state collection for compliance.\n- **Athena over CloudTrail** — SQL forensics over the log lake without a SIEM budget.\n- **incident response order** — isolate (revoke creds, quarantine security groups), eradicate, recover — with runbooks, not improvisation.\n- **permission boundary** — the maximum permissions a delegated admin can grant; caps their power.\n- **SCP (service control policy)** — org-level guardrail; bounds even root. SCPs restrict — they never grant.\n- **ABAC** — attribute-based access control: tag principals and resources, write one policy; scales where RBAC explodes.\n- **cross-account role** — temporary credentials for partner/third-party access; no long-lived keys, no rotation burden.\n- **WAF** — managed rules plus rate limiting; rate-based rules stop brute-force and scraping floods.\n- **Shield Advanced** — managed DDoS protection.\n- **Network Firewall** — VPC-level stateful inspection.\n- **PrivateLink** — access AWS services and partner services without traversing the internet.\n- **KMS envelope encryption** — the data key (DEK) encrypts the data; the KMS master key (KEK) encrypts the DEK — scale without KMS throttling.\n- **S3 Block Public Access** — set at the account level first; bucket policies are then defense in depth.\n- **S3 Object Lock** — immutability for compliance and log protection.\n- **Control Tower** — the account factory plus guardrails: the hardened landing zone.\n- **preventive guardrail** — SCPs (deny unencrypted S3, deny wrong regions): stops the bad action.\n- **detective guardrail** — Config rules and Security Hub findings: catches what slipped through."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Build the secure-by-default org, then prove you can investigate inside it.\n1. **Org trail.** Enable an org-wide CloudTrail into a dedicated log account with Object Lock on the bucket — the audit log attackers can't delete.\n2. **Aggregate.** Turn on the Config aggregator and Security Hub across the org; note one finding end to end.\n3. **Guardrails.** Apply preventive SCPs (deny unencrypted S3, deny deployments outside approved regions) and one detective Config rule; verify a violating action is blocked, not just flagged.\n4. **IAM depth.** Create a delegated admin with a permission boundary; write one ABAC policy using principal tags; grant a partner temporary access via a cross-account role — no access keys.\n5. **Data.** Enable account-level S3 Block Public Access; encrypt a bucket with a customer-managed KMS key (envelope encryption in action); run Macie on a test bucket.\n6. **Investigate.** Take a GuardDuty finding, pivot through Detective's graph to the root cause, and query the org trail with Athena for the full timeline.\n7. **Respond.** Execute the isolate → eradicate → recover runbook on a simulated compromised credential: revoke, quarantine the security group, rotate.",
     "code": "# Athena forensics over the org CloudTrail lake (no SIEM budget needed)\nSELECT eventtime, useridentity.arn, eventname, sourceipaddress\nFROM cloudtrail_logs\nWHERE eventname = 'ConsoleLogin'\n  AND eventtime > date_add('day', -30, current_date)\nORDER BY eventtime DESC\nLIMIT 100;\n\n# Preventive guardrail: deny unencrypted S3 org-wide (SCP)\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [{\n    \"Sid\": \"DenyUnencryptedS3\",\n    \"Effect\": \"Deny\",\n    \"Action\": \"s3:PutObject\",\n    \"Resource\": \"*\",\n    \"Condition\": { \"StringNotEquals\": { \"s3:x-amz-server-side-encryption\": \"aws:kms\" } }\n  }]\n}",
     "lang": "sql"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Stand up the hardened landing zone for a fictional company: org CloudTrail with Object Lock, Config aggregation, Security Hub, the preventive SCP set (deny unencrypted S3, deny unapproved regions) plus detective Config rules, account-level S3 Block Public Access, KMS envelope encryption on data stores, and the incident-response runbook (isolate → eradicate → recover) with one Detective-to-Athena investigation documented. Deliverables: the guardrail inventory (preventive vs detective), the IAM design (boundaries, one ABAC policy, cross-account pattern), and the investigation write-up. Graded on 'secure by default, compliant by construction' — every control must show its enforcement, not just its existence."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Delegate admin creation but cap their power?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Delegate admin creation but cap their power?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** permission boundaries\n- **Why:** Boundaries set the max — delegated admins can't exceed them. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Boundaries set the max — delegated admins can't exceed them.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in AWS Security Specialty: SCS-C03:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in AWS Security Specialty: SCS-C03 goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
