   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The AZ-104 glossary — identity first, then the platform.\n- **Entra ID** — the identity plane: users, groups (security vs Microsoft 365), devices (join/register), licenses.\n- **RBAC** — role-based access: Owner/Contributor/Reader plus granular roles; who can do what.\n- **RBAC scope** — management group → subscription → resource group → resource; assignments inherit downward.\n- **least privilege** — the standing rule for every assignment.\n- **PIM (Privileged Identity Management)** — just-in-time elevation: eligible assignments with approval, time-bound. Standing admin is the vulnerability.\n- **management group** — the hierarchy above subscriptions; assign policy and RBAC once, inherit everywhere.\n- **Azure Policy** — compliance as code: deny, audit, deployIfNotExists effects; governs what configurations are allowed.\n- **resource lock** — CanNotDelete / ReadOnly; CanNotDelete on production resource groups is the cheapest outage insurance.\n- **storage tiers** — Hot / Cool / Cold / Archive; cost follows access recency.\n- **redundancy ladder** — LRS (3 copies, 1 datacenter) → ZRS (3 zones) → GRS (+ paired region) → RA-GRS; durability and cost rise together.\n- **lifecycle management** — rules that tier aging blobs down automatically; storage cost is a policy, not a hope.\n- **SAS vs keys** — shared access signatures (scoped, expiring) versus storage account keys.\n- **availability set vs zone** — sets survive rack loss; zones survive datacenter loss. Zones are the resilience default.\n- **VMSS** — virtual machine scale sets: autoscaling VM fleets.\n- **NSG** — network security group: lowest rule number wins; defaults deny inbound internet.\n- **peering** — VNet peering; transit goes via the hub.\n- **Private Endpoint** — PaaS services (Storage, SQL) get VNet-private IPs; off the public internet.\n- **Load Balancer** — L4 regional distribution.\n- **Application Gateway** — L7 with WAF and path/host routing.\n- **Azure Monitor / Log Analytics** — metrics and logs; KQL queries over the workspace.\n- **Recovery Services vault** — Azure Backup policies and Site Recovery for DR."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Administer a real subscription, identity first.\n1. **Kill standing admin.** Move the admin accounts to PIM-eligible assignments with approval and time bounds; verify an elevation request and its audit trail.\n2. **RBAC by scope.** Create a custom role with least privilege, assign it at the resource-group scope, and confirm inheritance blocks at the subscription level.\n3. **Govern once.** Build the management-group hierarchy, assign an Azure Policy initiative (e.g. deny public storage) at the top, and confirm a violating deployment is denied.\n4. **Lock production.** Apply CanNotDelete locks to the production resource groups; attempt a delete and watch it fail safely.\n5. **Tier the storage.** Create a storage account, write a lifecycle rule (Hot → Cool → Archive by age), and compare the redundancy options on the durability/cost ladder.\n6. **Zone the compute.** Deploy across availability zones (not sets); put a VMSS behind the load balancer with autoscale.\n7. **Private the PaaS.** Add a Private Endpoint to the storage account; verify traffic no longer needs the public endpoint.\n8. **Query.** Learn five KQL queries against Log Analytics (CPU spikes, failed sign-ins, and three of your choosing) and save them; set one alert with an action group.",
     "code": "# KQL: CPU spikes over 5-minute windows\nAzureMetrics\n| where MetricName has 'Percentage CPU'\n| summarize avg(Average) by bin(TimeGenerated, 5m), Resource\n| where avg_Average > 80\n\n# KQL: failed sign-ins (identity is the control plane — watch it)\nSigninLogs\n| where ResultType != 0\n| summarize count() by UserPrincipalName, bin(TimeGenerated, 1h)\n| order by count_ desc",
     "lang": "kql"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Run a production-grade Azure subscription for a fictional team: Entra ID with PIM (no standing admin), RBAC custom roles at proper scopes, the management-group hierarchy with a policy initiative and production locks, zoned compute behind load balancing, lifecycle-managed storage, Private Endpoints on PaaS, and the five saved KQL queries with one alerting action group. Deliverables: the governance diagram (hierarchy → policy → locks), the identity design (PIM workflow documented), and the ops handover sheet. The AZ-104 grades administrators — this brief is the admin's portfolio."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Standing Global Admin for 50 admins. Fix?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Standing Global Admin for 50 admins. Fix?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** PIM eligible + approval + time-bound\n- **Why:** Eliminate standing privilege — PIM makes admin just-in-time and audited. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Eliminate standing privilege — PIM makes admin just-in-time and audited.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
