   "id": "il05-azure-architect",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "il05-azure-architect--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Spoke-to-spoke traffic blocked. Why?",
    "choices": [
     "DNS",
     "peering non-transitive — route via hub firewall",
     "policy",
     "NSG"
    ],
    "answer": 1,
    "explain": "Spoke peerings don't transit — hub NVA/firewall with UDRs enables it. — see 'Hands-on procedure' in this module's notes."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The architect's design vocabulary.\n- **landing zone** — the enterprise scaffold: platform subscriptions plus workload subscriptions under management groups, governed centrally.\n- **hybrid identity** — Entra Connect sync versus federation; B2B/B2C for external identity shapes.\n- **Access Reviews** — recurring, auditable certification of access; governance that doesn't rot.\n- **policy initiative** — bundled policies assigned at the management group for org-wide compliance.\n- **Log Analytics workspace strategy** — central versus regional workspaces; alert routing and workbooks for the business view.\n- **Azure SQL** — the relational, familiar choice.\n- **Cosmos DB** — global NoSQL with five consistency levels: Strong → Bounded Staleness → Session → Consistent Prefix → Eventual; latency and availability trade per level.\n- **hot/warm/cold path** — stream (Event Hubs) → serve (Cosmos DB) → analyze (Synapse): the lambda/kappa shapes.\n- **backup vs Site Recovery** — backup: RPO-hours, cheap. Site Recovery: replication with failover runbooks, RPO-minutes, for tier-1 workloads. The numbers choose.\n- **compute selection** — App Service vs AKS vs VMs vs Functions, chosen by workload shape.\n- **hub-spoke** — shared services in the hub (firewall, DNS, VPN), workloads in spokes; peering is non-transitive by default, so spoke-to-spoke routes via the hub.\n- **ExpressRoute vs VPN** — dedicated private circuit versus internet VPN; the SLA and consistency need decides.\n- **Azure Migrate** — assess → replicate → test cutover → cutover; migrations run in waves, never big-bang.\n- **design grading criterion** — rationale fit to constraints; defend the trade-offs, not just the choices."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Design in the order the exam asks: identity, data, continuity, infrastructure — then defend it.\n1. **Identity design.** Sketch the tenant shape (single vs B2B/B2C), hybrid approach (sync vs federation), and the PIM + Access Reviews governance loop.\n2. **Governance design.** Draw the management-group hierarchy (platform vs workload subscriptions), the policy initiatives, and the tagging + cost-management strategy.\n3. **Data design.** For each workload, pick by pattern: relational → Azure SQL; global NoSQL with tunable consistency → Cosmos DB (choose the level deliberately); blobs/files/queues/tables → Storage by pattern; analytics → Synapse/Fabric.\n4. **Continuity design.** Write the RTO/RPO per tier; assign backup (RPO-hours) or Site Recovery (RPO-minutes) accordingly — the numbers choose the tool.\n5. **Infrastructure design.** Select compute by workload, draw hub-spoke with the firewall and gateway in the hub, and plan the migration in waves with Azure Migrate.\n6. **Monitoring design.** Choose the workspace strategy (central vs regional), alert routing, and the workbooks the business will actually read.\n7. **Trade-off log.** For every major choice, record the constraint it serves (compliance? cost? operability?) and the rejected alternative — the exam grades the fit.",
     "code": "# Cosmos DB consistency choice (Session: the sweet spot for most global apps)\naz cosmosdb update \\\n  --name mycosmos --resource-group rg-data \\\n  --default-consistency-level Session\n\n# Hub-spoke: spoke UDR forces traffic through the hub firewall\naz network route-table route create \\\n  --resource-group rg-net --route-table-name spoke-rt \\\n  --name to-hub-firewall \\\n  --address-prefix 0.0.0.0/0 \\\n  --next-hop-type VirtualAppliance \\\n  --next-hop-ip-address 10.0.0.4",
     "lang": "bash"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Deliver the architecture capstone: a full design for a fictional company's Azure estate — requirements, constraints, service selections with rationale, network topology (hub-spoke with Private Endpoints), identity plan (hybrid + PIM + Access Reviews), business-continuity plan (RTO/RPO per tier), cost estimate, and the trade-off log. Deliverables: the design document, the topology diagram, and the constraint log where every choice cites compliance, cost, or operability. This is the AZ-305 in miniature: graded on rationale, where two good architects can disagree but both must defend."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Global app, 10ms reads worldwide, tunable consistency?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Global app, 10ms reads worldwide, tunable consistency?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Cosmos DB with Session consistency\n- **Why:** Cosmos DB: global distribution + five consistency levels — Session is the sweet spot. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Cosmos DB: global distribution + five consistency levels — Session is the sweet spot.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in Azure Solutions Architecture: AZ-305:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in Azure Solutions Architecture: AZ-305 goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
