   "id": "il05-azure-network",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "il05-azure-network--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "SQL PaaS must be VNet-private?",
    "choices": [
     "public + firewall",
     "service endpoint",
     "VPN",
     "Private Endpoint (Private Link)"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "Private Endpoint puts SQL on a VNet-private IP — off the public internet. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The network engineer's glossary — selection criteria included.\n- **VNet / subnet** — the isolated network and its segments; address space planning is forever — overlapping CIDRs with on-prem break peering and VPN permanently.\n- **NSG / ASG** — network security groups (with application security groups for grouping); stateful-ish rules, lowest number wins.\n- **peering** — VNet peering; transit via the hub, non-transitive by default.\n- **Private DNS zone** — VNet-linked, split-horizon name resolution for private networks.\n- **VPN Gateway** — S2S IPsec, P2S, and VNet-to-VNet connectivity over the internet.\n- **ExpressRoute** — a private dedicated circuit: consistent latency and throughput with an SLA, not internet variability.\n- **Virtual WAN** — Microsoft-managed hub-and-spoke at global scale; global transit without building your own hub mesh.\n- **Load Balancer** — L4 regional distribution.\n- **Application Gateway** — regional L7 with WAF and path/host routing.\n- **Front Door** — global L7 at the edge PoPs with instant failover and WAF; the global web-app answer.\n- **Traffic Manager** — DNS-based global routing; failover takes TTL time — not instant like Front Door.\n- **Private Link / Private Endpoint** — PaaS services get VNet-private IPs; the strong option for data-exfiltration control.\n- **service endpoint** — VNet identity for PaaS access; simpler, but the PaaS stays public-adjacent — weaker than Private Link.\n- **Azure Firewall** — L3–L7 filtering with FQDN rules and threat intelligence; egress control that actually works.\n- **DDoS Protection** — network-tier protection; enable on the VNet housing public IPs — the attack you can't afford to learn about live.\n- **Bastion** — browser-based RDP/SSH with no public IPs; replaces the internet-facing jumpbox — the most-attacked box in the estate."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Design and prove the network in constraint order: address, connect, deliver, secure.\n1. **Address once.** Plan non-overlapping CIDRs for hub, spokes, and on-prem; document the scheme — this decision is forever.\n2. **Hub-spoke.** Build the hub (firewall, VPN/ExpressRoute gateway, Bastion, Private DNS) and peer the spokes with gateway transit enabled; verify spoke-to-spoke flows through the hub.\n3. **Connect.** Stand up the S2S VPN, then compare against the ExpressRoute value proposition (consistent private throughput with SLA) for the workload's need.\n4. **Deliver.** Put the regional app behind Application Gateway (L7 + WAF + path routing); put the global app behind Front Door and test the instant failover; note Traffic Manager's TTL-time failover as the DNS-level alternative.\n5. **Private the PaaS.** Attach Private Endpoints to Storage and SQL; confirm with service endpoints disabled that exfiltration paths are closed.\n6. **Secure.** Write Azure Firewall FQDN rules (allow *.update.microsoft.com, deny the rest); enable DDoS Protection on the public-IP VNet; remove every jumpbox in favor of Bastion.\n7. **Select by constraint.** Drill ten scenarios: for each, name the deciding constraint (global? private? L7? instant failover?) and the service it picks — the AZ-700's actual skill.",
     "code": "# Hub-spoke peering with gateway transit (spokes use the hub's VPN/ER gateway)\naz network vnet peering create \\\n  --name spoke-to-hub --resource-group rg-net \\\n  --vnet-name spoke1 --remote-vnet hub \\\n  --allow-vnet-access --allow-forwarded-traffic \\\n  --use-remote-gateways\n\n# Private DNS: VNet-linked resolution for private endpoints\naz network private-dns zone create -g rg-net -n privatelink.database.windows.net\naz network private-dns link vnet create -g rg-net -z privatelink.database.windows.net \\\n  -n hub-link --virtual-network hub --registration-enabled false",
     "lang": "bash"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Design the network for a fictional company with on-prem datacenters on two continents: the addressing plan (non-overlapping, documented), hub-spoke topology with shared firewall and gateways, the connectivity choice per site (VPN vs ExpressRoute vs Virtual WAN with rationale), the delivery tier per app (Front Door vs App Gateway vs LB vs Traffic Manager), Private Link on all PaaS, and the security layer (Firewall FQDN egress, DDoS Protection, Bastion everywhere). Deliverables: the topology diagram, the addressing register, and the selection log — every service choice tied to its deciding constraint. The AZ-700 grades selection; this brief is the selection portfolio."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Global web app, instant regional failover, WAF?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Global web app, instant regional failover, WAF?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Front Door\n- **Why:** Front Door: global edge + instant failover + WAF — the global L7 answer. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Front Door: global edge + instant failover + WAF — the global L7 answer.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in Azure Network Engineering: AZ-700:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in Azure Network Engineering: AZ-700 goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
