   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The OpenShift glossary — admin and dev, side by side.\n- **project** — OpenShift's namespace plus membership and annotations; the multitenancy admin boundary.\n- **route** — OpenShift's built-in ingress; TLS modes are edge (terminate at the router), passthrough (terminate at the pod), reencrypt (both).\n- **S2I (Source-to-Image)** — git push → image → deploy: the developer on-ramp without Dockerfiles.\n- **Operator** — cluster extensions as code.\n- **IPI / UPI** — installer-provisioned versus user-provisioned installation shapes.\n- **quota / limit range** — per-project consumption bounds and defaults; the noisy-neighbor control for multitenancy.\n- **OCS/ODF** — the OpenShift storage offerings; StorageClasses for dynamic provisioning.\n- **day-2 operations** — upgrades, etcd backup, cert rotation, monitoring: the actual job after the day-1 install.\n- **DeploymentConfig** — the OpenShift-native deployment object alongside standard Deployments/StatefulSets.\n- **health probe** — liveness/readiness semantics carried into OpenShift deploy patterns.\n- **circuit breaker / bulkhead / retry with backoff** — the resilient-microservice patterns, via Service Mesh (Istio) or libraries.\n- **Quarkus** — 'supersonic subatomic Java': fast startup, low memory, native images via GraalVM for serverless; dev mode gives live reload — the inner loop that makes Java cloud-credible.\n- **twelve-factor** — codebase, dependencies, config (env, not code), backing services (attached resources), build/release/run, stateless processes, port binding, concurrency, disposability, dev/prod parity, logs (event streams), admin processes.\n- **container security baseline** — non-root user plus read-only filesystem where possible."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Administer the cluster, then ship on it — both exam personas in one flow.\n1. **Project and quota.** Create a project, set quotas and limit ranges, and prove a noisy-neighbor workload is contained.\n2. **S2I deploy.** Push application source and build with S2I — no Dockerfile — then expose it with a route; compare edge vs passthrough vs reencrypt TLS for a compliance requirement.\n3. **Health and config.** Add liveness/readiness probes, externalize config into ConfigMaps and secrets, and verify the same image runs in two environments via env-var config.\n4. **Resilience.** Add retries with backoff and a circuit breaker (mesh or library); kill a dependency and watch the bulkhead hold.\n5. **Day-2.** Take the etcd backup, rehearse cert rotation, and walk the upgrade pre-checks — installs are day 1, this is the career.\n6. **Twelve-factor audit.** Score the deployed app against all twelve factors; fix every violation.\n7. **Speed drill.** Redo the deploy tasks against the clock with `oc` — the performance exams grade live-cluster execution.",
     "code": "# S2I: source -> image -> deploy, no Dockerfile\noc new-project shop\noc new-app https://github.com/example/shop.git --strategy=source\noc expose svc/shop\n\n# Route TLS modes: edge | passthrough | reencrypt\noc create route passthrough shop-secure --service=shop --port=8443\n\n# Day-2: etcd backup before you touch anything\noc debug node/<node> -- chroot /host /usr/local/bin/cluster-backup.sh /home/core/assets/backup\n\n# Quotas: the noisy-neighbor control\noc create quota project-quota --hard=cpu=4,memory=8Gi,pods=20 -n shop",
     "lang": "bash"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Deliver the twelve-factor microservice on OpenShift: S2I or Dockerfile build, route with the compliance-correct TLS mode, probes, externalized config (same image in two environments), logs to stdout, horizontal scaling demonstrated — plus the admin side: project quotas, the etcd backup artifact, and a day-2 checklist (upgrades, cert rotation, monitoring). Deliverables: the deployed app, the twelve-factor audit with violations fixed, and the admin runbook. This is both exams in one portfolio: the admin proves the cluster is operable, the dev proves the app is shippable."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
