   "id": "il05-hashicorp-vault",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "il05-hashicorp-vault--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "Encrypt PII without managing keys?",
    "choices": [
     "Vault transit engine",
     "plaintext",
     "app crypto",
     "zip password"
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "explain": "Transit: send data, get ciphertext — Vault owns key lifecycle. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The secrets-and-mesh glossary.\n- **KV v2 secrets engine** — versioned static secrets.\n- **dynamic secrets** — leased, auto-revoked credentials (database, AWS STS): credentials that expire; the leaked-credential problem solved structurally.\n- **PKI secrets engine** — Vault as an internal certificate authority; short-lived certs.\n- **transit secrets engine** — encryption-as-a-service: Vault encrypts/decrypts, your data never lands in Vault; crypto without key-management pain.\n- **auth method** — how clients prove identity: userpass/LDAP (humans), OIDC (SSO), Kubernetes (pod service accounts), AWS (IAM-based) — machines authenticate as themselves.\n- **Vault policy** — HCL paths with capabilities (read, list, create, update, delete, sudo); deny-by-default — nothing is readable until a policy allows it.\n- **unseal** — Shamir key shards or auto-unseal via cloud KMS; plan this before production — unseal ceremonies fail at 3 AM otherwise.\n- **Raft integrated storage** — Vault's built-in storage backend; no Consul needed anymore. Back it up with `vault operator raft snapshot save`.\n- **DR vs performance replication** — disaster-recovery clusters versus read-scaling clusters.\n- **Consul catalog** — the registry of services.\n- **Consul DNS/HTTP** — `web.service.consul` and the HTTP API: the discovery interfaces.\n- **health check gating** — only healthy instances receive traffic; no load-balancer config needed.\n- **Consul Connect** — the service mesh: automatic, rotated mTLS between services.\n- **intention** — the allow/deny rule for service-to-service traffic (`consul intention create -allow web api`); the firewall rule for services, east-west microsegmentation for the mesh.\n- **gateways** — mesh, ingress, and terminating gateways for traffic in and out of the mesh.\n- **Consul KV vs Vault** — Consul KV is config, not encrypted by default; secrets belong in Vault."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Stand up secrets and mesh the way production does: unseal planned, policies least-privilege, discovery health-gated.\n1. **Init and unseal.** Initialize Vault, then configure auto-unseal via cloud KMS — operators should never handle key shards; rehearse the seal migration and rekey in staging.\n2. **Engines.** Enable KV v2 for static secrets, the database engine for dynamic creds, and transit for encryption-as-a-service.\n3. **Auth.** Configure OIDC for engineers (SSO) and the Kubernetes auth method for pods — humans via identity provider, machines as themselves.\n4. **Policies.** Write deny-by-default policies granting each service only its paths; verify a token can't read outside its policy.\n5. **Dynamic rotation.** Issue dynamic database credentials to an app; rotate the lease and confirm the app picks up new creds without restarts.\n6. **Raft backup.** Take a Raft snapshot; document the restore.\n7. **Consul.** Register services, query `web.service.consul`, add health checks and watch unhealthy instances drain from discovery automatically.\n8. **Mesh.** Enable Connect, create the `allow web → api` intention with default deny, and verify mTLS between the services; add a terminating gateway for the legacy dependency.\n9. **Monitor the critical path.** Alert on seal status, leader changes, and token-expiry storms — Vault is critical path; watch it like one.",
     "code": "# Dynamic database credentials: leased, auto-rotated, revoked on lease end\nvault secrets enable database\nvault write database/config/mydb \\\n  plugin_name=postgresql-database-plugin \\\n  connection_url=\"postgresql://{{username}}:{{password}}@db:5432/app\" \\\n  allowed_roles=\"app\"\nvault write database/roles/app db_name=mydb \\\n  creation_statements=\"CREATE ROLE \\\"{{name}}\\\" WITH LOGIN PASSWORD '{{password}}' VALID UNTIL '{{expiration}}';\" \\\n  default_ttl=\"1h\" max_ttl=\"24h\"\nvault read database/creds/app   # short-lived creds, no restarts to rotate\n\n# Transit: encryption without key-management pain\nvault secrets enable transit\nvault write -f transit/keys/orders\nvault write transit/encrypt/orders plaintext=$(echo -n '4111...' | base64)\n\n# Consul intention: the firewall rule for services\nconsul intention create -allow web api",
     "lang": "bash"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Build the secrets platform for a fictional microservices app: Vault with auto-unseal, KV + database + transit engines, OIDC for humans and Kubernetes auth for pods, deny-by-default least-privilege policies, Raft snapshots on schedule — and Consul with health-gated discovery plus a Connect mesh (intentions, mTLS, one gateway). Deliverables: the engine/auth/policy inventory, the dynamic-rotation demo (rotate without restarts), the intention map for the mesh, and the Vault monitoring checklist (seal status, leader changes, token-expiry storms). The brief proves the module's thesis: leaked credentials stop being an incident category when secrets are dynamic by default."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: App needs DB creds, rotation required, no restarts?",
     "body": "**Problem.** App needs DB creds, rotation required, no restarts?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Vault dynamic database secrets\n- **Why:** Dynamic: leased, auto-rotated, revoked on lease end — rotation without restarts. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Dynamic: leased, auto-rotated, revoked on lease end — rotation without restarts.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in Vault & Consul: Secrets and Service Networking:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in Vault & Consul: Secrets and Service Networking goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
