   "handsOnLab": {
    "id": "il05-cn-associates--lab",
    "title": "Platform slice: Cloud-Native Associate Stack",
    "brief": "You will build a thin but complete slice of Cloud-Native Associate Stack as code — provisioned, pipelined, secret-managed, cost-tagged, and torn down — because ClickOps doesn't count.",
    "steps": [
     "Define the slice in IaC: the resources, their configuration, and tags (owner, purpose, expiry).",
     "Store every secret in the secrets manager; verify none appear in code, logs, or state files.",
     "Build a CI pipeline with at least one test gate and one security scan gate before apply.",
     "Deploy, then verify end-to-end: the workload runs and the pipeline gates actually fired.",
     "Record the cost estimate and set a budget alert for the slice.",
     "Tear everything down with the destroy workflow; verify zero residual resources and confirm the bill stops."
    ],
    "deliverable": "IaC code, pipeline definition, cost estimate with budget alert screenshot or config, and teardown verification for Cloud-Native Associate Stack.",
    "checkpoint": "Deployed from code only, secrets managed, gates enforced, costs tracked, and teardown verified with no orphaned resources."
   },
   "id": "il05-cn-associates",
   "mockTask": {
    "id": "il05-cn-associates--mock",
    "type": "choice",
    "q": "PromQL: request rate from a counter?",
    "choices": [
     "avg(http_requests)",
     "sum(gauge)",
     "max()",
     "rate(http_requests_total[5m])"
    ],
    "answer": 3,
    "explain": "rate() over a _total counter with a window — the canonical query. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes."
   },
   "notes": [
    {
     "title": "Concepts",
     "body": "The associate-stack glossary — five exams, one vocabulary.\n- **KCNA** — Kubernetes and cloud-native fundamentals: architecture, orchestration concepts, microservices, immutable infrastructure, declarative APIs, GitOps concepts, observability basics. The on-ramp: take it before CKA if Kubernetes is new.\n- **KCSA** — cloud-native security associate: security overview, Kubernetes security fundamentals (RBAC, pod security, secrets, network policy), supply-chain basics. The prep ladder is KCSA → CKS.\n- **PCA (Prometheus)** — metrics types: counter, gauge, histogram, summary. The PromQL trap: rate() over counters, never over gauges.\n- **PromQL selectors and aggregation** — label selectors, rate(), and aggregation operators; alerting rules and the operator pattern.\n- **service discovery (Prometheus)** — automatic target discovery for scraping.\n- **CCA (Cilium)** — eBPF-powered networking: safe kernel programs replace kube-proxy iptables with eBPF maps.\n- **Hubble** — Cilium's observability layer.\n- **Cilium vs Calico policy depth** — Cilium enforces at L3–L7 (HTTP method/path-aware); Calico at L3/L4.\n- **CAPA (Argo)** — GitOps with Argo: declared desired state in git, agents converge the cluster; git is the source of truth, the cluster is a cache.\n- **Argo CD** — sync, drift detection, and automated sync policies; drift is detected, not debated.\n- **Argo Rollouts** — progressive delivery (canary/blue-green) as Kubernetes CRDs with metric analysis.\n- **sync waves/hooks** — ordering complex deploys: CRDs before the apps that need them.\n- **associate exam format** — proctored online multiple-choice: vocabulary and concepts. The performance depth lives at CKA/CKAD/CKS.\n- **recommended sequence** — KCNA → (PCA or CCA by interest) → CAPA → KCSA: breadth first, then the professional tier."
    },
    {
     "title": "Hands-on procedure",
     "body": "Build the breadth with light labs — vocabulary proven by doing, depth saved for the professional tier.\n1. **KCNA vocabulary drill.** Diagram the Kubernetes architecture from memory (control plane vs nodes), then check against the docs; repeat until it's instant.\n2. **PromQL.** Stand up Prometheus, scrape a test app, and run the canonical queries: `rate(http_requests_total[5m])`, aggregations by label, and one alerting rule. Deliberately run rate() over a gauge once — feel the trap, then never do it again.\n3. **Cilium.** Install Cilium with kube-proxy replacement, apply an L7 network policy (HTTP method/path), and observe it in Hubble; compare with a Calico-style L3/L4 rule.\n4. **Argo CD.** Point Argo CD at a git repo, watch drift detection flag a manual change, and let automated sync reconverge the cluster — git is truth, cluster is cache.\n5. **Rollouts.** Define a canary Rollout with analysis steps; watch the progressive delivery proceed on metrics.\n6. **KCSA map.** Walk the KCSA domain map against the CKS topics from Module 2; mark each CKS topic's associate-level prerequisite.\n7. **Sequence.** Sit the ladder in order — KCNA → PCA/CCA → CAPA → KCSA — drilling the domain maps and vocabulary for each.",
     "code": "# The canonical PromQL queries (rate over counters, never gauges)\nrate(http_requests_total[5m])\nsum by (service) (rate(http_requests_total[5m]))\nhistogram_quantile(0.99, sum by (le) (rate(http_request_duration_seconds_bucket[5m])))\n\n# Argo CD: the app of apps pattern — git as the source of truth\nargocd app create platform \\\n  --repo https://github.com/example/gitops.git \\\n  --path apps --dest-server https://kubernetes.default.svc \\\n  --sync-policy automated --auto-prune\n\n# Argo Rollout: canary as a declarative resource\nkubectl argo rollouts promote web   # manual gate in the canary steps",
     "lang": "bash"
    },
    {
     "title": "Project brief",
     "body": "Assemble the associate breadth portfolio: the KCNA architecture diagram drawn from memory, the PromQL query sheet (ten queries including the gauge trap documented), the Cilium L7 policy with Hubble evidence, the Argo CD gitops repo with drift-detection demonstrated, one Rollout canary, and the KCSA→CKS prerequisite map. Deliverables: the query sheet, the policy manifests, the gitops repo link, and the exam-sequence plan (KCNA → PCA/CCA → CAPA → KCSA) with a domain-map checklist per exam. This is the breadth layer — when you later sit the 2-hour performance exams, the vocabulary will already be reflex."
    },
    {
     "title": "Honesty note",
     "body": "This module is original preparation material. Interstitium Labs confers no certificate and is not affiliated with AWS, Microsoft, HashiCorp, Red Hat, CNCF, or the Linux Foundation. Exam codes and versions change (codes below are as of 2026-10-07) — confirm the current objectives on the vendor's site before booking. Labs, scenarios, and questions are original practice — never recalled exam content."
    },
    {
     "title": "Worked example: Cluster state drifted from git. GitOps response?",
     "body": "**Problem.** Cluster state drifted from git. GitOps response?\n\n**Step 1 — Answer from first principles.** Cover the choices and commit to an answer using only the concept, not pattern-matching. Say your reasoning out loud — in interviews and in real work, the reasoning is what gets graded.\n\n**Step 2 — Reveal and verify.**\n- **Correct answer:** Argo CD detects drift; sync (auto or manual) reconverges\n- **Why:** Drift detection + sync — the cluster converges back to git's declared state. — see 'Concepts' in this module's notes.\n\n**Step 3 — Lock in the principle.** Compress the explanation into one reusable rule: *Drift detection + sync — the cluster converges back to git's declared state.* Write it on a card. If you cannot state the rule without the item in front of you, you memorized the item instead of the idea.\n\n**Step 4 — Teach it back.** Explain this item to an imaginary junior in three sentences: what was asked, what the right move was, and the one mistake to avoid. Teaching is the retention test — if the explanation wobbles, re-study before moving on."
    },
    {
     "title": "Pitfalls & how to dodge them",
     "body": "Cloud-native work fails in predictable ways. Guard against these in Cloud-Native Associate Stack:\n- **ClickOps.** Console changes with no IaC behind them. If it isn't in code, it didn't happen — and it will drift by Friday.\n- **Secrets in code, env files, or chat.** Every secret in Cloud-Native Associate Stack goes through the secrets manager from day one. Rotating a leaked credential costs ten times preventing the leak.\n- **No cost guardrails.** Cloud bills are silent until they aren't. Tag everything, set budget alerts, and know the price of the architecture you just drew.\n- **Skipping teardown.** Labs and experiments that never get destroyed become permanent, billable, unpatched infrastructure. `destroy` is part of the workflow, not an afterthought.\n- **Untagged, unnamed resources.** 'test-final-v2-REAL' at 3 AM during an incident is how outages get longer. Naming and tagging are operational controls.\n- **Pipeline without gates.** Shipping straight to production with no test, scan, or approval stage turns every commit into a potential incident."
    }
   ],
